Unnecessary Comparison Identified.

Redundant Boolean Comparison in `require` Statements
'''
The contract contains two explicit comparisons of a boolean return value against `true`:

- In `mintSynth`:
  `require(iSYNTHFACTORY(_DAO().SYNTHFACTORY()).isSynth(synthOut) == true, "!synth");`
- In `burnSynth`:
  `require(iSYNTHFACTORY(_DAO().SYNTHFACTORY()).isSynth(synthIN) == true, "!synth");`

Because `isSynth()` already returns `bool`, the `== true` check is redundant and can be simplified to:

```solidity
require(iSYNTHFACTORY(_DAO().SYNTHFACTORY()).isSynth(synthOut), "!synth");
require(iSYNTHFACTORY(_DAO().SYNTHFACTORY()).isSynth(synthIN), "!synth");
```

This makes the code cleaner, reduces unnecessary boolean comparisons, and avoids potential misunderstanding of the intended condition.
'''

Redundant Comparison in `addRevenue`
'''
In the `addRevenue` function:

```solidity
if(!(revenueArray.length == 2)){
    revenueArray.push(_totalRev);
} else {
    addFee(_totalRev);
}
```

The expression `!(revenueArray.length == 2)` is a redundant comparison. It can be written more directly as:

```solidity
if(revenueArray.length != 2){
    revenueArray.push(_totalRev);
} else {
    addFee(_totalRev);
}
```

This simplifies the conditional logic and improves readability without changing behavior.
'''
Detector: UnnecessaryComparisonDetector
Classification: code-quality (no standard SWC)
